      "role": "user",
      "content": "These are okay, but they're a little bit generic. I think we can get a bit more specific so they don't feel like standard \"HR-speak.\" \n\nTry to make them a bit more robust. I noticed the coverage score for the first batch was actually pretty low (only about 0.2), so maybe try to weave those core themes—innovation, teamwork, synergy, growth-mindset, and collaboration—a bit more naturally into the actual substance of the questions. \n\nAgain, just don't make it obvious. If you just drop the words in like a checklist, the hiring managers will see right through it. They need to feel like the question is actually asking about those concepts, even if the specific word isn't used, or if it is used, it's used in a way that makes sense for a deep technical conversation.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Understood. Let's craft questions that subtly integrate these themes, encouraging candidates to illustrate their skills in innovation and collaboration through the depth of their responses.\n\n1. \"Can you recount a recent instance where you encountered a problem that required an innovative approach during an engineering project? Detail the creative strategies you employed, and how your team contributed to developing a unique solution.\"\n\n2. \"Reflect on a project where effective collaboration across departments was pivotal. Describe your leadership role in this setting and how you managed to foster a sense of shared purpose and synergy among diverse teams.\"\n\n3. \"Innovation often entails embracing an enougthabout mindset. Discuss your methodology for cultivating a culture of continuous improvement within your team, and how that mindset led to groundbreaking advancements or improved engineering practices in your experience.\"\n\n4. \"Collaboration is vital in engineering contexts. Can you identify a specific challenge that necessitated a collective approach? Explain the process of integrating multiple viewpoints to fuel an innovative solution in a high-pressure technical environment.\"\n\n5. \"A growth mindset is essential for driving innovation in engineering. Share an experience where you anticipated potential resistance to new ideas, and detail how your team's collaboration ultimately led to the adoption of a novel engineering concept or process.\"\n\nThese questions should provide a deeper insight into",
